  "evidences": [
    {
      "id": "ev_7",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s2"
      },
      "text": "GRABNEW MALWARE POS MALWARE FIN6 is a cyber criminal group intent on stealing payment card data for monetization.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_18",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p5_s6"
      },
      "text": "Previously, we observed another FIN group — FIN2 — leverage several existing Citadel compromises to deploy their custom tools and expand within a network to compromise payment card systems.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_19",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p5_s7"
      },
      "text": "Likewise, Proofpoint recently observed GRABNEW variants leading to downloads of POS malware known as AbaddonPOS.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_24",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p5_s12"
      },
      "text": "PoSeidon, a variant of the Backoff POS malware.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_34",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 6,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p6_s8"
      },
      "text": "The tools targeted CVE-2013-3660, CVE-2011-2005 and CVE-2010-4398, all of which could allow local users to access kernel-level privileges.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_38",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 6,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p6_s12"
      },
      "text": "of Characters After locating POS systems within the target’s environment, FIN6 deployed POS malware that we call TRINITY.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_39",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 7,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p7_s1"
      },
      "text": "In addition to collecting credentials, FIN6 used publicly available tools to map the internal network and conduct reconnaissance against Active Directory, Structured Query Language (SQL) servers and NetBIOS.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_40",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 7,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p7_s2"
      },
      "text": "In particular, during the reconnaissance phase they gathered information on systems running SQL instances, dumping schemas for multiple databases and SQL user accounts.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_41",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 7,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p7_s3"
      },
      "text": "Specific tools used by FIN6 included Microsoft’s built-in SQL querying tool (osql.exe), Query Express (a free, portable graphical SQL client capable of connecting to Microsoft SQL and Oracle databases) and AdFind, a free command-line tool for querying Active Directory.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_42",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 7,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p7_s4"
      },
      "text": "Over the course of one day, for example, the group targeted more than 900 SQL servers to dump reconnaissance information to support further operations.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_45",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 7,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p7_s7"
      },
      "text": "After locating POS systems within the target’s environment, FIN6 deployed POS malware that we call TRINITY (also known as FrameworkPOS), with Scheduled Tasks being used for persistence.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_66",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Fireeye_rpt-fin6(04-20-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 7,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p7_r1"
      },
      "text": "In addition to collecting credentials, FIN6 used publicly available tools to map the internal network and conduct reconnaissance against Active Directory, Structured Query Language (SQL) servers and NetBIOS. In particular, during the reconnaissance phase they gathered information on systems running SQL instances, dumping schemas for multiple databases and SQL user accounts. Specific tools used by FIN6 included Microsoft’s built-in SQL querying tool (osql.exe), Query Express (a free, portable graphical SQL client capable of connecting to Microsoft SQL and Oracle databases) and AdFind, a free command-line tool for querying Active Directory. Over the course of one day, for example, the group targeted more than 900 SQL servers to dump reconnaissance information to support further operations.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
